#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152957} :activity/id #uuid "4d0ed4da-8ca5-550b-8192-8108992e2e42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050689 :topic/id #uuid "5f54d743-67b1-5023-a4c2-42de103b3b6d" :topic/math-academy-id 2675} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153453 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for $k\\neq 0,$ we can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $k\\neq 0,$ we have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0.$ For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." :tutorial/id #uuid "80aec645-9a36-5a86-a866-dedff95e8091" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9019 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "105aa43c-c166-5fbc-95f3-769e0f6da6c1" :step/math-academy-id 154648 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152958}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151843 :question/id #uuid "9f2134e8-62dd-5748-a202-819b4ca5208f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050678}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415052}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"} :step/id #uuid "ac11f463-25aa-55b9-9ac0-a331f041dc4f" :step/math-academy-id 154649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153452 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1.$ \n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1,$ we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n\n$$\n6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2  \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1,$ we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n\n$$\n4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." :tutorial/id #uuid "b53a59cc-bae8-5427-90c1-8c6ec21119a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9018 :tutorial/title "The Second Division Property"} :step/id #uuid "1b9d1463-a78c-5122-b686-fb1e6073e599" :step/math-academy-id 154652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151842 :question/id #uuid "c638e469-4c15-52f3-a397-a7aac86d654e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048141}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415053}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"} :step/id #uuid "da4afd2b-3aae-5054-a973-b9b456caa174" :step/math-academy-id 154653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151858 :question/id #uuid "344211e7-11c0-54f9-8a78-e64ff53deff0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415049}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"} :step/id #uuid "f823d7c8-2ace-5f65-b27c-2b9e00e71538" :step/math-academy-id 154657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153454 :tutorial/content "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn) & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,k(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,\\cancel{k}n\\,|\\,\\cancel{k}(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n).$ Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,k(a-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n,$ we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n.$ Now, using the definition again, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\,|\\,(a-b) & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe50ad20-f759-5095-acdc-f9e5e8d0926d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9021 :tutorial/title "Proving the Division Properties"} :step/id #uuid "10408cde-12fb-5b1c-9dcd-ef994ab66bbb" :step/math-academy-id 154660}] :activity/title "The Division Properties of Modular Arithmetic" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152342}]}
